                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I'm having trouble understanding how to power electromagnets. Can you explain it to me step by step?\n\nProfessor: Sure, let's start with the basics. Electromagnets work by creating a magnetic field through the flow of electric current.\n\nStudent: Okay, so how do we create that electric current?\n\nProfessor: There are a few ways to do it, but the most common method is to run a continuous current through a wire.\n\nStudent: How do we run a continuous current through a wire?\n\nProfessor: We can do that by connecting the wire to a power source, such as a battery or a power supply.\n\nStudent: What kind of wire should we use?\n\nProfessor: Any wire that conducts electricity will work, but it's important to choose a wire with a low resistance to minimize energy loss.\n\nStudent: Once we have the wire and power source, what do we do next?\n\nProfessor: We need to wrap the wire around a core material, such as iron, to create a coil. The more turns the wire makes around the core, the stronger the magnetic field will be.\n\nStudent: So, we have the coil, but how do we make it an electromagnet?\n\nProfessor: We need to connect the ends of the wire to a switch or a circuit that can control the flow of current. When the switch is closed, the current flows through the wire and creates a magnetic field around the coil.\n\nStudent: I see. And what about the options listed in the original question - running wire through currants or currently running wire through?\n\nProfessor: I think those are typos. The correct options are running wire through a core material (option C) or running a continuous current through the wire (option B).",
