                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Original question: What can cause loose soil?\n\nHelpful knowledge: Loose soil refers to soil that is not compact and has a low density. It can be caused by various factors such as erosion, weathering, and biological activity.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is loose soil?\n2. What are the factors that can cause loose soil?\n\nAnswering sub-question 1: Loose soil refers to soil that is not compact and has a low density. It can be easily moved or shifted by external forces such as wind or water.\n\nAnswering sub-question 2: The factors that can cause loose soil include erosion, weathering, and biological activity. Erosion can be caused by wind or water, which can remove the top layer of soil and leave behind loose soil. Weathering can also cause soil to become loose as a result of physical or chemical breakdown of the soil particles. Biological activity, such as the burrowing of worms or other animals, can also loosen soil. \n\nAnswering the original question: Loose soil can be caused by erosion, weathering, and biological activity such as the burrowing of worms or other animals. Therefore, option C, a worm burrowing through the earth, can cause loose soil. Option A, a koala sitting on a tree, and option D, a bird flying through the air, are not likely to cause loose soil. Option B, none of these, is incorrect as there are factors that can cause loose soil.",
