                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Dogs shiver for various reasons, including cold temperatures, fear, anxiety, excitement, and illness. The time of day may not necessarily be a determining factor in a dog's likelihood to shiver.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What factors contribute to a dog's likelihood to shiver?\n2. Are there any patterns or trends in a dog's shivering behavior based on the time of day?\n\nAnswering sub-question 1: \nFactors that contribute to a dog's likelihood to shiver include cold temperatures, fear, anxiety, excitement, and illness. Cold temperatures can cause a dog to shiver as a way to generate body heat. Fear and anxiety can also cause shivering as a physical response to stress. Excitement can cause shivering as a result of an adrenaline rush. Illnesses such as fever or pain can also cause shivering.\n\nAnswering sub-question 2:\nThere is no clear pattern or trend in a dog's shivering behavior based on the time of day. A dog may shiver at any time of day if they are experiencing any of the factors mentioned above. The time of day is not a determining factor in a dog's likelihood to shiver. Therefore, none of the options provided in the original question can be definitively chosen as the correct answer.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "8c37a90f-c602-4c7d-a1fb-3030b4923446",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
