                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Neville Longbottom is a character in the Harry Potter series. He is a Gryffindor student at Hogwarts School of Witchcraft and Wizardry and is known for his bravery and loyalty to his friends.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What acts of courage did Neville perform as a child?\n2. What acts of courage did Neville perform as an adult?\n3. How do these acts of courage compare in terms of difficulty and risk?\n4. Is there any evidence in the books to suggest that Neville has become less courageous as he has grown older?\n\nAnswers:\n1. As a child, Neville showed courage by standing up to his friends and standing up to his enemies. He also faced his fears by standing up to his grandmother and facing the boggart in the third year.\n2. As an adult, Neville showed courage by joining Dumbledore's Army and fighting in the Battle of Hogwarts. He also showed loyalty to his friends by refusing to hand over Harry Potter to Voldemort.\n3. The acts of courage that Neville performed as an adult were more difficult and risky than those he performed as a child. Joining Dumbledore's Army and fighting in the Battle of Hogwarts put Neville in danger of being captured or killed by the Death Eaters.\n4. There is no evidence in the books to suggest that Neville has become less courageous as he has grown older. In fact, his acts of courage as an adult show that he has become even more brave and loyal to his friends. Therefore, the answer to the original question is false.",
