                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, we need to define what a theocratic government is and examine the historical context of the Great Pyramids.\n\nA theocratic government is a system of government in which priests rule in the name of God or a god. In ancient Egypt, the pharaoh was considered a god-king and held both political and religious power. However, it is unclear whether the construction of the Great Pyramids was directly overseen by the pharaoh or by a group of priests.\n\nThe Great Pyramids were built during the Old Kingdom period of ancient Egypt, specifically during the reigns of Pharaohs Khufu, Khafre, and Menkaure. These pharaohs were known for their ambitious building projects, including the construction of the pyramids. The construction of the pyramids required a large workforce and extensive resources, including food, water, and building materials.\n\nWhile the pharaohs held significant power and authority in ancient Egypt, it is unlikely that they oversaw every aspect of the construction of the pyramids. Instead, it is more likely that a group of skilled architects, engineers, and laborers were responsible for the actual construction, with oversight from the pharaoh or a group of priests.\n\nTherefore, the answer to the question \"Were the Great Pyramids built by a theocratic government?\" is unclear. While the pharaohs held significant religious and political power in ancient Egypt, it is unclear whether they directly oversaw the construction of the pyramids or whether a group of priests were involved.",
