                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, did the Qwerty keyboard layout predate computers?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's true.\n\nStudent: How did it come about then?\n\nProfessor: The Qwerty keyboard layout was actually designed for typewriters in the late 1800s.\n\nStudent: Why was it designed specifically for typewriters?\n\nProfessor: The layout was designed to prevent the keys from jamming. The most commonly used letters were placed far apart from each other to prevent the type bars from colliding with each other.\n\nStudent: So, it was designed for efficiency?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's right. The Qwerty layout was designed to increase typing speed and reduce the likelihood of jamming.\n\nStudent: Did the Qwerty layout change when computers were invented?\n\nProfessor: No, the Qwerty layout remained the same when computers were invented. It was already a well-established standard and changing it would have been difficult and costly.\n\nStudent: So, even though there are more efficient keyboard layouts available now, we still use Qwerty?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct. Despite the availability of more efficient layouts, the Qwerty layout remains the most widely used keyboard layout in the world.",
