                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I have a question. Are blue lips normal?\n\nProfessor: Well, it depends on the context. Can you give me more information?\n\nStudent: Sure. I noticed that sometimes my lips turn blue when I'm really cold or when I'm exercising. Is that normal?\n\nProfessor: Ah, I see. In that case, it's actually quite common for lips to turn blue in those situations. When you're cold, your body constricts blood vessels in your extremities to conserve heat, which can reduce blood flow to your lips and make them appear blue. Similarly, when you exercise, your body needs more oxygen, and if you're not getting enough, your lips can turn blue as a result.\n\nStudent: Okay, that makes sense. But what if my lips are blue all the time, even when I'm not cold or exercising?\n\nProfessor: That could be a sign of a more serious medical condition, such as cyanosis, which is when your body isn't getting enough oxygen. In that case, you should see a doctor right away.\n\nStudent: Got it. So, to answer the original question, are blue lips normal? \n\nProfessor: In some situations, yes, but if they're happening all the time, then no. It's important to pay attention to your body and seek medical attention if you're concerned.",
