                        "cot": " An anatomical heart is a heart that has been removed from the body, while a symbolic heart is an image or representation of a heart. So, the first difference is that one is a real, physical object, and the other is an image. But what about the way they look?\n\nAn anatomical heart would likely look different than a symbolic heart because it is a real, physical object. It would have the same general shape as a heart, but it would be missing some of the smaller details that are often included in symbolic hearts. For example, an anatomical heart would not have the small lines that represent the valves, and it would not have the small, pointy shape at the bottom. Instead, it would be a more simplified version of a heart.\n\nSymbolic hearts, on the other hand, can take on many different forms. Some may look very similar to anatomical hearts, while others may be more stylized or abstract. In general, though, symbolic hearts are usually more detailed than anatomical hearts, and they often include additional elements that are not found on real hearts.",
