                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: A lapidary is someone who cuts, polishes, and engraves gemstones and other precious materials. Geologists study the physical and chemical properties of rocks, minerals, and other materials that make up the Earth's crust.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What materials does a lapidary work with?\n2. What materials do geologists study?\n3. Is there any overlap between the materials that lapidaries work with and the materials that geologists study?\n\nAnswering the sub-questions:\n1. A lapidary works with gemstones and other precious materials, such as diamonds, rubies, sapphires, and emeralds. They may also work with semi-precious stones like amethyst, turquoise, and garnet.\n2. Geologists study rocks, minerals, and other materials that make up the Earth's crust. This includes igneous, sedimentary, and metamorphic rocks, as well as minerals like quartz, feldspar, and mica.\n3. Yes, there is overlap between the materials that lapidaries work with and the materials that geologists study. Many gemstones and precious materials are formed from minerals that are also studied by geologists. For example, diamonds are formed from carbon, which is a mineral that geologists study. Lapidaries may also work with rocks and minerals that are commonly studied by geologists, such as quartz and feldspar.\n\nAnswer: True, a lapidary does work with items that are studied by geologists.",
