                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Stars are formed from clouds of gas and dust called nebulae. Gravity plays a crucial role in the formation of stars.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the process of star formation?\n2. How does gravity affect the gas and dust particles in a nebula?\n3. What are the different ways in which gravity can influence the formation of stars?\n\nAnswer:\n1. The process of star formation begins with a cloud of gas and dust called a nebula. \n2. Gravity causes the gas and dust particles in the nebula to be pulled together. As more and more particles come together, the gravitational force becomes stronger, causing the particles to condense into a denser and denser region. \n3. Eventually, the pressure and temperature in the center of the region become so high that nuclear fusion begins, and a star is born. Therefore, the statement that best describes the role of gravity in the formation of stars is B) Gravity causes gases and dust particles to condense into spheres.",
