                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Planets and moons are celestial bodies in our solar system. Planets are larger than moons and orbit the Sun, while moons orbit planets.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is a planet?\n2. What is a moon?\n3. How do planets differ from moons in terms of size?\n4. How do planets differ from moons in terms of orbit?\n5. How do planets differ from moons in terms of atmosphere?\n6. Can moons support life?\n\nAnswers:\n1. A planet is a celestial body that orbits the Sun, is spherical in shape, and has cleared its orbit of other debris.\n2. A moon is a celestial body that orbits a planet.\n3. Planets are generally larger than moons.\n4. Planets orbit the Sun, while moons orbit planets.\n5. Planets have atmospheres, while moons do not.\n6. It is possible that some moons could support life, but none have been confirmed to do so yet.\n\nAnswer to original question: C) All planets orbit the Sun; all moons orbit planets.",
