                    {
                        "id": "ec04dbfa-88a1-406a-992b-f9d4d35f138f",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-17",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I have a question about the tides. I noticed that sometimes the water rises really high at the beach and other times it barely covers the pillars under the pier. What causes these differences in water level?\n\nProfessor: Ah, good question. These differences in water level are primarily due to the gravitational influence of which of the following: A) the Sun, B) the Moon, C) asteroids, or D) comets.\n\nStudent: Hmm, I'm not sure. Can you explain each option?\n\nProfessor: Sure. The Sun and the Moon both have gravitational pulls on the Earth. The Moon is actually the primary cause of tides because it is much closer to the Earth than the Sun. Asteroids and comets are not significant enough to cause tides.\n\nStudent: Okay, so it's either the Sun or the Moon. But how exactly do they cause tides?\n\nProfessor: The gravitational pull of the Moon causes a bulge of water on the side of the Earth facing the Moon, and another bulge on the opposite side of the Earth. These are called high tides. The areas in between these two bulges experience low tides. As the Earth rotates, the bulges move around the planet, causing the tides to change throughout the day.\n\nStudent: That makes sense. But what about the Sun's gravitational pull?\n\nProfessor: The Sun also has a gravitational pull on the Earth, but it is much weaker than the Moon's. When the Sun, Moon, and Earth are aligned, their combined gravitational pull causes higher high tides and lower low tides, which are called spring tides. When the Sun and Moon are at right angles to each other, their gravitational pulls partially cancel each other out, causing lower high tides and higher low tides, which are called neap tides.\n\nStudent: I think I understand now. So the answer is B) the Moon.\n\nProfessor: Correct! The Moon is the primary cause of tides on Earth.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "e12d89e5-2c61-4b90-b8d2-0ac1ae4bd55a",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
