                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, can you explain what happens when a star explodes?\n\nProfessor: Sure, when a star explodes, it releases a tremendous amount of energy and creates an intensely bright object.\n\nStudent: What is this object called?\n\nProfessor: It's called a supernova.\n\nStudent: How is a supernova different from a nova?\n\nProfessor: A nova is a smaller explosion that occurs on the surface of a star, while a supernova is a much larger explosion that destroys the entire star.\n\nStudent: What about red giants and white dwarfs? Are they related to supernovas?\n\nProfessor: Yes, red giants and white dwarfs are both types of stars that can eventually become supernovas. Red giants are large, aging stars that have exhausted their fuel and expanded in size, while white dwarfs are small, dense stars that have exhausted their fuel and collapsed.\n\nStudent: So, when a red giant or a white dwarf becomes a supernova, does it always create a supernova?\n\nProfessor: Not necessarily. It depends on the mass of the star. Only stars that are at least eight times more massive than the sun can become supernovas. Smaller stars will either become white dwarfs or neutron stars.",
