                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Plan:\n1. Define weathering and its effects on rocks.\n2. Identify the types of rocks and their characteristics.\n3. Determine which type of rock is most likely to form by weathering.\n\nAnswer:\n1. Weathering is the process by which rocks are broken down into smaller pieces by physical, chemical, or biological means. This can occur due to exposure to wind, water, ice, or living organisms. Weathering can cause rocks to become weaker and more susceptible to erosion.\n2. There are three main types of rocks: igneous, sedimentary, and metamorphic. Igneous rocks form from the cooling and solidification of magma or lava. Sedimentary rocks form from the accumulation and cementation of sediment, which can include pieces of other rocks. Metamorphic rocks form from the alteration of existing rocks due to heat, pressure, or chemical reactions.\n3. Based on the characteristics of each type of rock, it is most likely that sedimentary rock forms by weathering. This is because weathering breaks down existing rocks into smaller pieces, which can then be transported and deposited as sediment. Over time, this sediment can become compacted and cemented together to form sedimentary rock.",
                        "answers": [
