                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain the question again, please?\n\nProfessor: Sure. When sediment is deposited on the ocean floor, what happens next in the formation of sedimentary rocks?\n\nStudent: Okay, got it. So, what are the options?\n\nProfessor: The options are A) heating, B) burying, C) eroding, and D) weathering.\n\nStudent: Hmm, I think it might be burying. Is that correct?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct! When sediment is deposited on the ocean floor, it gets buried under more and more layers of sediment. Over time, the weight of the overlying sediment compresses the lower layers, causing them to harden and turn into sedimentary rock.\n\nStudent: Oh, I see. So, why isn't it weathering?\n\nProfessor: Weathering is the process of breaking down rocks into smaller pieces. It's actually the first step in the formation of sedimentary rocks, but it doesn't happen after sediment is deposited on the ocean floor. Instead, it happens on land, where rocks are exposed to the elements like wind, rain, and temperature changes.\n\nStudent: That makes sense. And what about heating?\n\nProfessor: Heating is actually a process that happens much later in the formation of sedimentary rocks, during a process called metamorphism. This is when rocks are subjected to high temperatures and pressures, causing them to change form. But it doesn't happen right after sediment is deposited on the ocean floor.\n\nStudent: Okay, I understand now. Thank you for explaining it so clearly!",
                        "answers": [
