                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Forests are important carbon sinks, meaning they absorb and store carbon dioxide (CO2) from the atmosphere through photosynthesis. When forests are destroyed, the stored carbon is released back into the atmosphere.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What happens to the stored carbon in a forest when it is destroyed?\n2. What gas is released into the atmosphere when carbon is released?\n3. Does the release of this gas contribute to climate change?\n\nAnswers:\n1. When a forest is destroyed, the stored carbon is released back into the atmosphere in the form of carbon dioxide (CO2).\n2. The gas released into the atmosphere when carbon is released is carbon dioxide (CO2).\n3. Yes, the release of carbon dioxide (CO2) contributes to climate change as it is a greenhouse gas that traps heat in the atmosphere.",
