                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Habitat destruction refers to the process of natural habitats being damaged or destroyed, leading to the displacement or extinction of species. Therefore, an adaptation resulting from habitat destruction would be a change in behavior or physical characteristics of a species that allows it to survive in the altered habitat. \n\nA) A bird building its nest in a water tower: This could be an adaptation to habitat destruction if the bird's natural nesting sites were destroyed, and the water tower provided a suitable alternative. However, it could also be a natural behavior of the bird to build nests in elevated locations, regardless of habitat destruction. \n\nB) A rabbit's fur turning white in the winter: This is an example of seasonal camouflage, which is a natural adaptation to changing environmental conditions. It is not directly related to habitat destruction. \n\nC) A lizard shedding its skin: This is a natural process that allows lizards to grow and replace damaged skin. It is not directly related to habitat destruction. \n\nD) A salmon swimming upstream: This is a natural behavior of salmon during their spawning season, and is not directly related to habitat destruction. \n\nTherefore, the most likely adaptation resulting from habitat destruction would be A) a bird building its nest in a water tower, as it could be a response to the loss of natural nesting sites.",
