                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Adaptations are traits that organisms develop over time to help them survive in their environment. Habitat destruction refers to the process of natural habitats being destroyed or altered by human activities such as deforestation, urbanization, and pollution.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is habitat destruction?\n2. How do organisms adapt to habitat destruction?\n3. Which of the given options is an adaptation that resulted from habitat destruction?\n\nAnswering sub-questions:\n1. Habitat destruction refers to the process of natural habitats being destroyed or altered by human activities such as deforestation, urbanization, and pollution.\n2. Organisms can adapt to habitat destruction by changing their behavior, physiology, or morphology. For example, they may change their feeding habits, move to a new location, or develop new physical features to help them survive in the altered environment.\n3. Option A, a bird building its nest in a water tower, is the most likely adaptation that resulted from habitat destruction. This is because the bird has had to change its behavior and find a new place to build its nest due to the destruction of its natural habitat. The other options are not necessarily adaptations resulting from habitat destruction. Option B, a rabbit's fur turning white in the winter, is a natural adaptation to seasonal changes. Option C, a lizard shedding its skin, is a natural process that helps the lizard grow and maintain healthy skin. Option D, a salmon swimming upstream, is a natural behavior that salmon exhibit during their spawning season.",
