                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: What is the term used to describe the area through which a plane travels that contains gases, liquids, and solids?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n\n- The area through which a plane travels that contains gases, liquids, and solids is not limited to any specific layer or level of the Earth.\n- The term \"atmosphere\" refers to the layer of gases that surrounds the Earth, which includes the air through which a plane travels, as well as other gases like ozone and carbon dioxide.\n- The term \"lithosphere\" refers to the solid outer layer of the Earth, which includes the crust and upper mantle, and does not include the air through which a plane travels.\n- The term \"biosphere\" refers to the part of the Earth where living organisms exist, which includes the air, water, and land where life can be found, but does not specifically refer to the area through which a plane travels.\n- The term \"hydrosphere\" refers to the part of the Earth where water exists, which includes oceans, lakes, rivers, and groundwater, but does not specifically refer to the area through which a plane travels.\n\nTherefore, the best description for the area through which a plane travels that contains gases, liquids, and solids is the atmosphere.\n\nAnswer: A) atmosphere.",
