                    {
                        "id": "fc1730d8-efeb-4308-b0f2-11fe277e0a83",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": " The summit of Mount Everest is 8,848 meters (29,029 feet) above sea level. This is much higher than the troposphere, which is the lowest layer of the atmosphere and extends up to about 10 kilometers (6.2 miles) above the Earth's surface. The stratosphere is the next layer, and it extends up to about 50 kilometers (31 miles). The mesosphere is the layer above the stratosphere, and it extends up to about 85 kilometers (53 miles). The thermosphere is the highest layer of the atmosphere, and it extends up to about 600 kilometers (370 miles).\n\nSince the summit of Mount Everest is much higher than 10 kilometers, it is not in the troposphere. Since it is not higher than 50 kilometers, it is not in the stratosphere. Since it is not higher than 85 kilometers, it is not in the mesosphere. Therefore, the summit of Mount Everest is in the thermosphere.\n\nAnswer: C) thermosphere",
