                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nFirst, we need to understand the layers of the atmosphere and their characteristics:\n\n1. Troposphere: This is the layer closest to the Earth's surface, extending up to about 7-20 km. It is where weather occurs and temperature decreases with altitude.\n\n2. Stratosphere: This layer extends from the top of the troposphere to about 50 km. It contains the ozone layer, which absorbs harmful UV radiation from the sun. Temperature increases with altitude in this layer.\n\n3. Mesosphere: This layer extends from the top of the stratosphere to about 85 km. Temperature decreases with altitude in this layer.\n\n4. Thermosphere: This is the outermost layer of the atmosphere, extending from about 85 km to 600 km or more. Temperature increases with altitude in this layer due to the absorption of solar radiation.\n\nNow, let's apply this knowledge to the question. We know that the temperature decreases as altitude increases on Mount Everest. This means that the summit of the mountain must be in the troposphere or the stratosphere, as these are the layers where temperature decreases with altitude. However, we also know that Mount Everest is the highest mountain in the world, with a summit height of 8,848 meters. This means that the summit is well above the typical height of the troposphere (7-20 km) and even the stratosphere (up to 50 km). Therefore, we can eliminate options A and B (mesosphere and stratosphere) and narrow it down to options C and D (thermosphere and troposphere).\n\nThe answer is D) troposphere.",
