                        "cot": "Step 1: Determine the altitude of Mount Everest's summit.\nMount Everest's summit is approximately 8,848 meters (29,029 feet) above sea level.\n\nStep 2: Identify the altitude ranges of the different atmospheric layers.\n- Troposphere: 0 to 8-15 km (0 to 5-9 miles) above sea level, depending on latitude and season.\n- Stratosphere: 15 to 50 km (9 to 31 miles) above sea level.\n- Mesosphere: 50 to 85 km (31 to 53 miles) above sea level.\n- Thermosphere: 85 to 600 km (53 to 372 miles) above sea level.\n\nStep 3: Determine which atmospheric layer Mount Everest's summit falls into.\nSince the summit of Mount Everest is 8,848 meters (29,029 feet) above sea level, it falls within the troposphere, which extends up to 8-15 km (5-9 miles) above sea level.\n\nAnswer: D) troposphere",
