                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: In which atmospheric layer is the summit of Mount Everest located, given that the temperature decreases as altitude increases?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- We know that the temperature decreases as altitude increases on Mount Everest.\n- This means that the summit of Mount Everest must be located in a layer of the atmosphere where temperatures decrease with altitude.\n- The atmospheric layers are defined by their temperature profiles, with the troposphere being the lowest layer where temperature decreases with altitude, followed by the stratosphere, mesosphere, and thermosphere.\n- Therefore, the summit of Mount Everest must be located in either the troposphere, stratosphere, mesosphere, or thermosphere.\n- We can eliminate the mesosphere and thermosphere as options, since they are both characterized by increasing temperatures with altitude.\n- This leaves us with the troposphere and stratosphere as possible options.\n- The summit of Mount Everest is approximately 8,848 meters (29,029 feet) above sea level, which is well within the troposphere (which extends up to about 12 km or 7.5 miles).\n- Therefore, the summit of Mount Everest is located in the troposphere.\n\nAnswer: D) troposphere",
