                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain how tree roots can break apart rock?\n\nProfessor: Sure. When a tree root grows into a crack in a rock, it can exert pressure on the sides of the crack. As the root continues to grow, the pressure increases and can eventually cause the rock to break apart.\n\nStudent: And how does water freezing in rock cracks cause it to break apart?\n\nProfessor: When water gets into a crack in a rock and then freezes, it expands. This expansion puts pressure on the sides of the crack, causing it to widen. Over time, this can cause the rock to break apart.\n\nStudent: So, how are these two processes alike?\n\nProfessor: Both of these processes are forms of weathering. Weathering is the process by which rocks are broken down into smaller pieces. In the case of tree roots and freezing water, they both cause physical forces to act on the rock, which can lead to its breakdown. So, the correct answer is D) Both are forms of weathering.",
                        "answers": [
