                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, can you please explain which weather event is most likely to cause a forest fire?\n\nProfessor: Sure, the most likely weather event to cause a forest fire is a lightning strike.\n\nStudent: Why is that?\n\nProfessor: Lightning strikes can ignite dry vegetation and start a fire. In fact, lightning is responsible for about 50% of all forest fires.\n\nStudent: What about flash floods? Can they cause forest fires?\n\nProfessor: No, flash floods actually have the opposite effect. They can put out forest fires by saturating the ground and vegetation with water.\n\nStudent: And what about tornadoes and hailstorms?\n\nProfessor: Tornadoes and hailstorms are also unlikely to cause forest fires. Tornadoes can uproot trees and damage vegetation, but they don't typically start fires. Hailstorms can damage vegetation, but they don't generate enough heat to start a fire.\n\nStudent: Thank you, Professor. That makes sense.",
