                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain why the force of a hurricane decreases as it moves over land?\n\nProfessor: Sure. The main reason is that the supply of warm, moist air decreases.\n\nStudent: Why does the hurricane need warm, moist air?\n\nProfessor: Hurricanes are fueled by warm, moist air rising from the ocean's surface. As the air rises, it cools and condenses, releasing heat energy that powers the storm.\n\nStudent: So, what happens when the hurricane moves over land?\n\nProfessor: When the hurricane moves over land, it loses its source of warm, moist air. Land doesn't have as much moisture as the ocean, so the hurricane weakens as it moves inland.\n\nStudent: Does anything else happen when the hurricane moves over land?\n\nProfessor: Yes, as the hurricane moves over land, it encounters more friction from the ground. This friction slows down the winds and weakens the storm even further.\n\nStudent: So, which answer choice best explains why the force of a hurricane diminishes as it moves over land?\n\nProfessor: The correct answer is D) The supply of warm, moist air decreases.",
