                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I heard on the radio that the outside temperature is 32\u00b0C. What type of weather should I expect when I step outside?\n\nProfessor: Well, 32\u00b0C is a pretty high temperature. So, you can expect hot weather.\n\nStudent: How did you know that?\n\nProfessor: Generally, when the temperature is above 30\u00b0C, it's considered hot weather. So, 32\u00b0C is definitely hot.\n\nStudent: Okay, that makes sense. But what about the other options? Why aren't they correct?\n\nProfessor: The other options are not correct because they describe weather conditions that are not associated with high temperatures. For example, cool, rainy fall weather and cold, snowy winter weather are associated with low temperatures. And spring weather with flowers blooming can be warm or cool, but it's not typically hot.\n\nStudent: I see. So, the answer is C) hot, sunny summer weather.\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct. When the temperature is 32\u00b0C, you can expect hot, sunny summer weather.",
