                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what happens when light hits a mirror?\n\nProfessor: Sure. When light hits a mirror, it can either be absorbed, transmitted, refracted, or reflected.\n\nStudent: So, which one of those options is the correct answer?\n\nProfessor: The correct answer is B) reflected. When light hits a mirror, it bounces off the surface and reflects back.\n\nStudent: Why doesn't the light get absorbed or transmitted?\n\nProfessor: The surface of the mirror is designed to be very smooth and reflective, so the light doesn't get absorbed or transmitted. Instead, it bounces off the surface and reflects back.\n\nStudent: What about refraction? What does that mean?\n\nProfessor: Refraction is when light passes through a material and changes direction. This can happen when light passes through a lens or a prism, for example.\n\nStudent: So, why doesn't refraction happen when light hits a mirror?\n\nProfessor: Refraction doesn't happen when light hits a mirror because the surface is so smooth and flat that the light doesn't pass through it. Instead, it bounces off the surface and reflects back.",
