                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, can you explain the concept of potential energy in relation to a roller coaster?\n\nProfessor: Sure, potential energy is the energy that an object possesses due to its position or state. In the case of a roller coaster, it is the energy that the coaster has due to its height above the ground.\n\nStudent: Okay, so in this question, the roller coaster is climbing up the highest hill on its track. So, where will the front car of the coaster most likely have its greatest potential energy?\n\nProfessor: The answer would be B) at the top of the highest hill. This is because the roller coaster has gained the most height at this point, and therefore, it has the most potential energy.\n\nStudent: Why is it not at the bottom of the highest hill?\n\nProfessor: At the bottom of the highest hill, the roller coaster has lost most of its potential energy and has converted it into kinetic energy, which is the energy of motion.\n\nStudent: And why is it not at the start or end of the track?\n\nProfessor: At the start of the track, the roller coaster has not gained any height yet, so it has no potential energy. At the end of the track, the roller coaster has lost all of its potential energy and has converted it into kinetic energy, which is used to propel the coaster to the end of the track.",
