                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, how does the speed of radio waves compare with the speed of infrared waves?\n\nProfessor: Well, radio waves and infrared waves are both types of electromagnetic waves, but they have different frequencies. The speed of an electromagnetic wave depends on its frequency and the medium it's traveling through.\n\nStudent: So, does that mean they travel at different speeds?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct. In general, radio waves travel at the same speed as light, which is about 186,000 miles per second. Infrared waves, on the other hand, travel slightly slower than light, at around 186,000 miles per second.\n\nStudent: Okay, so radio waves are faster?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's right. Radio waves are faster than infrared waves.\n\nStudent: And what about the wave speed varying with frequency?\n\nProfessor: Ah, yes. That's also true. The speed of an electromagnetic wave does vary with its frequency. In general, higher frequency waves travel faster than lower frequency waves. But for radio and infrared waves, the difference in frequency is not enough to make a significant difference in their speeds.",
