                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Sandy soils have a low water-holding capacity and are well-draining, which means that water quickly moves through the soil and is not retained for long periods of time. This can make it difficult for plants to obtain the necessary nutrients and moisture for growth.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are the characteristics of plants that can live in sandy soils?\n2. What are the environmental factors that plants in sandy soils must be able to withstand?\n\nAnswering sub-question 1:\nPlants that can live in sandy soils typically have the following characteristics:\n- Deep root systems that can reach water and nutrients deeper in the soil\n- Drought-tolerant adaptations, such as the ability to close stomata (pores on leaves) to reduce water loss\n- Low nutrient requirements, as sandy soils typically have low levels of organic matter and nutrients\n- Ability to tolerate high temperatures, as sandy soils can become very hot in direct sunlight\n\nAnswering sub-question 2:\nPlants in sandy soils must be able to withstand dry conditions, as water is not retained in the soil for long periods of time. Therefore, option D (dry conditions) is the correct answer. While plants that can live in sandy soils may also have adaptations to withstand other environmental factors, such as high temperatures or low nutrient levels, the ability to tolerate dry conditions is the most important factor for survival in sandy soils.",
