                        "cot": "How do hills differ from mountains?\nA) Hills are smaller and have a more rounded shape.\nB) Hills are not as deep and are more level than mountains.\nC) Hills are not as steep and rocky as mountains.\nD) Hills are not as tall and do not have snow on them like mountains.\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- The question is asking for the ways in which hills are different from mountains.\n- Option A states that hills are smaller and have a more rounded shape, which is a clear difference from mountains.\n- Option B suggests that hills are deeper and more level, but this does not make sense as hills are typically not deep and are often described as rolling or undulating.\n- Option C states that hills are steeper and rockier, which is not true as mountains are typically steeper and rockier than hills.\n- Option D suggests that hills are taller and snowy, which is also not true as mountains are typically taller and have snow on them.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A) Hills are smaller and have a more rounded shape.",
