                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Hills and mountains are both landforms that rise above the surrounding terrain, but they differ in their size, shape, and geological features.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are the defining characteristics of hills?\n2. What are the defining characteristics of mountains?\n3. How do hills and mountains differ in terms of size?\n4. How do hills and mountains differ in terms of shape?\n5. How do hills and mountains differ in terms of geological features?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Hills are typically smaller and more rounded than mountains. They have a gentle slope and are often covered in grass or trees.\n2. Mountains are larger and more rugged than hills. They have steep slopes and are often rocky and barren.\n3. Hills are generally shorter than mountains, with an elevation of less than 1,000 feet. Mountains, on the other hand, can reach heights of over 10,000 feet.\n4. Hills have a more gradual slope than mountains, which have steep sides and a pointed peak.\n5. Mountains are often formed by tectonic activity, such as the collision of two plates or the uplift of a landmass. Hills, on the other hand, can be formed by erosion, deposition, or volcanic activity.",
