                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, can you explain to me the ways in which hills are different from mountains?\n\nProfessor: Sure, there are several ways in which hills are different from mountains. Which one would you like me to explain?\n\nStudent: Can you explain option A, that hills are smaller and more rounded?\n\nProfessor: Of course. Hills are generally smaller in size compared to mountains and have a more rounded shape. They are usually less than 1,000 feet in height and have a gentle slope. Mountains, on the other hand, are much taller and have a more pointed shape.\n\nStudent: That makes sense. Can you explain option B, that hills are deeper and more level?\n\nProfessor: Actually, that option is not correct. Hills are not deeper than mountains. In fact, they are usually shallower and have a more level surface. Mountains, on the other hand, have a more rugged and uneven surface.\n\nStudent: Oh, I see. Can you explain option C, that hills are steeper and rockier?\n\nProfessor: That option is also not correct. Hills are generally less steep than mountains and have a smoother surface. Mountains, on the other hand, are much steeper and have a more rocky surface.\n\nStudent: Okay, I understand. Can you explain option D, that hills are taller and snowy?\n\nProfessor: That option is also not correct. Hills are usually not tall enough to have snow on them, and they do not have permanent snow caps like mountains do. Mountains are much taller and have a colder climate, which allows for snow to accumulate on their peaks.\n\nStudent: Thank you, professor. I understand the differences between hills and mountains now.",
